1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,791
This is Dot Goose, part one. We will follow eight
small C sharp programs through the Goose

2
00:00:06,791 --> 00:00:07,440
compiler.

3
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:14,099
For each program, compare the source and the
generated code, then look at what both versions

4
00:00:14,099 --> 00:00:15,163
actually print.

5
00:00:15,758 --> 00:00:20,379
This program prints Dot Goose, starts with forty,
and adds two.

6
00:00:20,509 --> 00:00:25,734
The C sharp integer becomes I thirty two in
Goose, and Write Line becomes print.

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,642
The condition still checks for forty-two before
printing the value.

8
00:00:30,772 --> 00:00:37,368
Both runs also print the wrapped result from the
final addition. We will unpack that in chapter

9
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:37,715
four.

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,133
Look at the strings between the quotation marks.

11
00:00:41,263 --> 00:00:47,837
Only the call changes. The escape sequences for
the tab, quotes, backslash, and line breaks carry

12
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:48,380
through.

13
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:54,458
The first print writes an empty line. Later,
carriage return and newline remain distinct.

14
00:00:54,588 --> 00:00:59,871
The test compares raw output bytes, so even a
change a terminal might hide would fail.

15
00:01:00,467 --> 00:01:06,562
The index starts at zero and stops before five.
The running total starts at negative two.

16
00:01:06,692 --> 00:01:12,416
At index two, the else branch changes the Boolean
flag instead of adding to the total.

17
00:01:13,008 --> 00:01:18,225
The remaining additions take the total to six.
The final condition checks the total and the

18
00:01:18,225 --> 00:01:18,511
flag.

19
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:22,926
Goose keeps the same loop and branch structure.
Both runs print six.

20
00:01:23,508 --> 00:01:28,280
This example adds one to the largest signed
thirty-two-bit integer.

21
00:01:28,410 --> 00:01:33,437
Unchecked C sharp arithmetic wraps around, giving
the smallest signed integer.

22
00:01:34,008 --> 00:01:40,133
The Dot Goose helper adds the same bits as
unsigned values, then casts back to a signed

23
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:40,696
integer.

24
00:01:40,826 --> 00:01:46,178
That preserves all three results even when Goose
runs with debug checks enabled.

25
00:01:46,758 --> 00:01:51,735
The scoped Counter class becomes a Goose struct.
The factory named new Counter takes the

26
00:01:51,735 --> 00:01:52,923
constructor argument.

27
00:01:53,053 --> 00:01:59,172
Add still looks like a method call. Its writable
receiver lets it update the counter in place.

28
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:04,507
Point is a value type. Copying it gives the
program an independent value.

29
00:02:04,637 --> 00:02:10,801
Changing the copy leaves the original sum at
thirteen. The copy prints five, after the counter

30
00:02:10,801 --> 00:02:11,916
prints forty-two.

31
00:02:12,508 --> 00:02:18,863
The explicit Point constructor sets X and marks
the point ready. The parameterless construction

32
00:02:18,863 --> 00:02:19,799
uses defaults.

33
00:02:19,929 --> 00:02:23,981
That means zero for both coordinates and false
for the ready flag.

34
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:30,091
Changing the blank point's Y coordinate does not
set its flag. Its Value method still returns

35
00:02:30,091 --> 00:02:30,866
negative one.

36
00:02:30,996 --> 00:02:37,288
The assigned copy can change independently. The
final values are seven, negative one, and nine.

37
00:02:37,883 --> 00:02:44,086
Point and Counter each declare a method called
Read. The receiver type tells the compiler which

38
00:02:44,086 --> 00:02:45,000
method to use.

39
00:02:45,130 --> 00:02:49,403
Goose represents those methods as functions with
distinct receiver types.

40
00:02:49,967 --> 00:02:56,608
Point reads seven. Counter reads twenty-one, and
Read Twice calls the counter version two times.

41
00:02:56,738 --> 00:03:02,280
Setting the enabled flag to false changes the
counter to negative one. Both runs produce the

42
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:03,123
same sequence.

43
00:03:03,717 --> 00:03:09,533
The counter starts at ten. The left side of the
sum calls Next first, printing eleven.

44
00:03:09,663 --> 00:03:16,107
Then the two arguments to Take call Next again,
printing twelve and thirteen in that order.

45
00:03:16,675 --> 00:03:22,143
Take prints its first argument, twelve, and
returns its second argument, thirteen.

46
00:03:22,273 --> 00:03:26,348
The sum prints twenty-four, and the counter still
holds thirteen.

47
00:03:26,478 --> 00:03:31,555
Matching only the final value would miss a
reordered print. The test compares the entire

48
00:03:31,555 --> 00:03:32,074
sequence.

49
00:03:32,675 --> 00:03:38,889
All eight programs matched in the test suite,
both as Goose executables and in Goose debug

50
00:03:38,889 --> 00:03:39,235
mode.

51
00:03:39,365 --> 00:03:44,194
You can find the source, the compiler, and the
tests in the Dot Goose repository.
